<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!-- The table a sample rack stands on: a clone of AWS RoboMaker's
     aws_robomaker_residential_CoffeeTable_01, vendored here so the mission owns
     its own props.

     WHY A CLONE AND NOT THE ORIGINAL. Four of these are mission furniture -
     three collection points and one delivery point - and mission props are
     spawned by the launch rather than written into aws_hospital.sdf. Pointing
     four spawns at a model that also appears in the world file would make it
     impossible to tell, later, which instance is scenery and which is a
     destination. The meshes are copied in rather than referenced across model
     directories because the map generator resolves a mesh URI by globbing under
     the model's OWN directory (see collision_triangles), so a cross-model
     model:// URI would silently produce a table with no collision geometry.

     WHY THIS MODEL AND NOT A TALLER, MORE HOSPITAL-LOOKING ONE. Table height is
     not decoration here - it decides whether the arm can reach the rack at all.
     A rack is grasped by a block 0.170 m above its own tray, so a top at height
     h puts the grasp point at h + 0.170; the FR3's base sits 0.3837 m above the
     floor, 0.0799 m ahead of base_link, with 0.855 m of reach, and the base
     parks with the payload 0.78 m ahead of base_link. Measured across every
     model in this world:

       this table          top 0.32   ->  0.708 m of reach,  83%
       drawer              top 0.63   ->  0.815 m,           95%
       surgicaltrolley     top 0.70   ->  0.852 m,          100%   fully extended
       adjtable            top 0.81   ->  0.920 m,          108%   out of reach
       bedtable            top 1.06   ->  1.098 m,          128%   out of reach

     0.32 m is within a couple of centimetres of the 0.30 m box it replaces, so
     every constant tuned against that geometry - CLAW_STOP_X, TABLE_X_OFFSET,
     the front camera's horizon analysis - carries over untouched.

     GEOMETRY, measured off the collision mesh rather than taken from the model
     card: 1.327 x 0.668 m, flat top at z = 0.3238, origin centred on the
     footprint with the feet at z = 0. So a rack spawns at z = 0.3238 and stands
     on the top; see rack_table_layout.py for how far in from the near edge, and
     why the bumper decides that number.

     STATIC, as the original is. These never move, and a static model costs the
     solver nothing. -->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="rack_table">
    <static>1</static>
    <link name="link">
      <inertial>
        <mass>21.7</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.81375</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>2.76675</iyy><iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>3.254</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <collision name="collision">
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>model://rack_table/meshes/aws_CoffeeTable_01_collision.DAE</uri>
            <scale>1 1 1</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <contact><ode><max_vel>0.01</max_vel></ode></contact>
          <!-- A rack standing on this must not creep. The original carries no
               friction block at all, which leaves it on Gazebo's default mu;
               0.7 is plastic on a painted top, the same value the rack's own
               tray uses, so neither surface is the limiting one. -->
          <friction><ode><mu>0.7</mu><mu2>0.7</mu2></ode></friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <visual name="visual">
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>model://rack_table/meshes/aws_CoffeeTable_01_visual.DAE</uri>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
